HBO's Watchmen Forge its its own path  but it also doesn't forget where it came  from  it's packed full of covert references  throughout its first season so if you're  curious here are some of the Easter eggs  you might have missed in what might be a  meta joke about Easter eggs Watchmen  features a ton of actual eggs which came  first the chicken or the egg both some  serve as references to the comic series  for example in the first episode as  Angela a bar breaks up egg yolks in a  bowl they formed the smiley face you  might recognize from the cover of the  first issue of Watchmen there are plenty  of other eggs throughout the season that  are more generally symbolic dr.  Manhattan conjures and drinks one during  his first date with Angela in the fourth  episode we see a couple that gets some  much-needed fertility help from the  mysterious lady true spilling eggs on  their porch and during the series finale  Angela finds one egg left unbroken among  the others she smashed to the ground  earlier in the evening dr. Manhattan  left that egg behind after his death as  a way of transferring his powers angela  consumes the egg but whether she has his  powers is left ambiguous let's jump back  to the smiley face image naturally show  creator Damon Lindelof and his crew  found other ways to work the iconic  visual into the show particularly the  blood spatter on the comedian's yellow  button the first episode of Watchmen  opens on a horrific depiction of the  Tulsa Race Riot of 1921 which remains  one of the most devastating instances of  racial violence in US history white  citizens attacked Greenwood a primarily  african-american neighborhood looting  robbing and killing with abandon in  watchman's universe descendants of the  attack were paid reparations causing  resentment among white supremacists in  that sequence the show sneaks in a  terrible tragic reminder of the button a  blood spatter on the head of young will  Reeves that perfectly mimics the smiley  face a similar blood splatter can be  spotted later in the same episode on  Judds badge during his hanging watchmen  takes place in a world quite different  from ours  where Nixon never resigned and Robert  Redford was the president that followed  him a lot is different culturally -  including the popular reading material  Mac Shea's pirate yarn tales of the  black freighter is prominent  featured and excerpted in the original  comic that explains why there are so  many references to pirates in the show  specifically a detective named pirate  Jenny a costumed aunt by one of Angela's  children a skull and crossbones wax seal  on the game wardens letter a pirate flag  the name of Laurie's hotel and more  several characters are also spotted  reading a book called fog dancing in  quick blink and you'll miss it shots  another novel written by the prolific  fictional Shae seems like it's pretty  good too would you let me give you  another book when a young Angela looks  at a series of VHS tapes in the show's  seventh episode one of them is a  Hollywood adaptation of Fogg dancing  both versions of niteowl were major  characters in The Watchmen comic but his  story doesn't get much play in the show  the show is however rife with actual  owls and references to the nocturnal  Birds Laurie even has a pet owl what's  his name oh the owl who that's his name  Angela's other daughter dresses as an  owl and the kids also have a few owl  toys visible on their dresser plus when  Angela Dawn's night vision goggles  she looks eerily like night owl himself  the first night owls famous tell-all  book under the hood also makes a cameo  in the first episode a copy of the book  is on doomed sheriff Judd Nelson's desk  when Angela stops by his office and  during their subsequent chat she sips  coffee from an owl shaped mug one of the  big questions in the early episodes of  the season was where dr. Manhattan was  hiding was he on Mars as widely believed  or somewhere else as it turned out he  was hiding in plain sight as Calais bar  supportive loving husband to Angela  after the two fell in love dr. Manhattan  took on the appearance of a regular  human and had Angela wiped his memory so  he could live as normal a life as  possible at least for a while there are  plenty of clues leading up to Cal's big  reveal other characters are skeptical  about Cal's accident that left him with  amnesia it's a car crash right I've done  a lot of research on memory total  amnesia is extremely rare and he and  Laurie a former lover of dr. Manhattan  seemed to have a special connection  right off the bat in the 8th episode we  learn how he and Angela met two very  specific  musical cues accompany dr. Manhattan  throughout the episode mr. blue by the  Fleetwood's and a riff on George  Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue  in the Watchmen universe the u.s. wins  the Vietnam War decisively thanks to dr.  Manhattan's help and President Richard  Nixon is exalted as a hero Vietnam  becomes the 51st state and Nixon is  remembered as an American icon  throughout the series Viet Nam's  inclusion in the United States is  addressed rather broadly angela grows up  there and saigon is mentioned repeatedly  as an american city during Judd  Crawford's funeral the flag draped over  his coffin looks very different from the  real flag and it buries 51 stars during  the show's pilot when Looking Glass  interrogate SAS suspect inside a  mysterious chamber that shows  alternating patriotic and disturbing  images Nixon's face can be seen on a  revised Mount Rushmore the bar in Saigon  where Angela and dr. Manhattan first  meet is called mr. Edie's bar a direct  reference to the comics and the site of  one of its most horrific events Eddie  Blake also known as the comedian took up  with a Vietnamese woman during the war  but when she became pregnant he lost  interest in her after the unnamed woman  slashed Blake's face with a beer bottle  leaving a scar he shot her in the face  at mr. Eddie's and killed her the  director of the episode  Nicole Cassell told polygon that the  show's production crew used two  descriptions from the comic to inform  what the interior of mr. Eddie's would  look like  dr. Manhattan explains towards the end  of the season that everything that will  happen has either already occurred or is  happening right now making every outcome  inevitable  he's also notably the son of a  watchmaker as such there's a clock motif  that runs through Watchmen connecting  dr. Manhattan's thought process and  wisdom to Adrian Veidt Veidt who spends  years imprisoned on Europa with cloned  servants created by dr. Manhattan as his  only company writes a play for the  clones to perform called the watchmaker  son it's a retelling of dr. Manhattan's  origin story though it burns one of the  clones to death rather than giving him  superpowers beyond that there are plenty  of clocks in the show all of which  reference the comics Doomsday Clock as  well as its final cover which itself  depicts a bloody clock check out one of  our newest videos right here Plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  shows are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

